# Accessibility Standards

## Form Labels Must Be Programmatically Associated

Every `<input>`, `<select>`, and `<textarea>` must have an associated label via `htmlFor`/`id` or `aria-label`:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
<label htmlFor="signup-email">Email</label>
<input id="signup-email" type="email" />

// ✅ GOOD — for inputs without visible labels
<input aria-label="Search nganyas" type="search" />

// ❌ BAD — label not associated
<label>Email</label>
<input type="email" />
```

## Error Messages Must Use `role="alert"`

Dynamic error text must be announced to screen readers:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
{error && <p role="alert" className="text-red-300">{error}</p>}

// ❌ BAD — not announced
{error && <p className="text-red-300">{error}</p>}
```

## Icon-Only Buttons Need `aria-label`

Buttons containing only an icon must have an accessible name:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
<button aria-label="Go back"><ChevronLeft /></button>

// ❌ BAD — no accessible name
<button><ChevronLeft /></button>
```

## Clickable Divs Must Be Keyboard-Accessible

If a `<div>` has `onClick`, it needs `role="button"`, `tabIndex={0}`, and keyboard handling:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
<div role="button" tabIndex={0} onClick={handleClick}
  onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') handleClick(); }}>

// ❌ BAD — not focusable or keyboard-accessible
<div onClick={handleClick}>
```

Prefer using `<button>` instead of `<div>` when possible.

## Modals Must Have Focus Management

Modal/overlay components must:
1. Set `aria-labelledby` pointing to the title element (use `useId()` for unique IDs)
2. Move focus into the modal on open (`dialogRef.current?.focus()`)
3. Handle Escape key to close
4. Use `role="dialog"` and `aria-modal="true"`
